---
import RenewalGuide from "../../components/astro/RenewalGuide.astro";
const props = {
  title: "How to auto-create renewal deals in HubSpot (and why workflows break)",
  description: "Plan a HubSpot renewal workflow with reviewed dates, native record creation, line-item checks and duplicate prevention. Test the next renewal cycle before launch.",
  h1: "How to make renewal deals create themselves in HubSpot",
  intro: "Start with one customer agreement and define the next-term opportunity it should create. HubSpot workflows can create deals; the work is choosing the right source, dates, owner and repeat behavior for your process.",
  steps: [
    { n: 1, t: "Define the eligible agreement and planning event", body: "Choose the source object and renewal route. Require reviewed term dates, an agreement identity and an owner. A Closed Won trigger can suit initial planning, but do not use Close Date plus 365 days as a universal contract renewal rule." },
    { n: 2, t: "Configure native record creation first", body: "HubSpot's <a href='https://knowledge.hubspot.com/workflows/create-records-with-workflows'>Create record documentation</a> covers deal creation, property copying, owners and associations. It also documents adding product-based line items. Test whether those controls meet your specification before choosing an app or custom integration." },
    { n: 3, t: "Verify dates, ownership and line items", body: "Set the intended renewal pipeline and stage, reviewed next-term dates and responsible owner. Check the company and agreement associations. Adding a catalog product does not establish that negotiated source prices, discounts and terms were copied. Reconcile the target against the accepted agreement." },
    { n: 4, t: "Test duplicates and the next renewal cycle", body: "Use an agreement-plus-next-term identity to check whether a target already exists. Test a repeated trigger, concurrent creators and missing inputs. Follow HubSpot's <a href='https://knowledge.hubspot.com/workflows/add-re-enrollment-triggers-to-a-workflow'>re-enrollment guidance</a> for the selected triggers; do not assume a universal year-three stop or a universal workaround." },
  ],
  burden: "A renewal workflow needs an accountable owner and a recovery path. Check what happens when a term changes, a record is enrolled twice or creation succeeds before a later step fails. A retry should identify the existing target before attempting another creation.",
  bridge: "Bring one agreement, the intended next-term deal and a failed or repeated event to the discovery call. Use those examples to decide whether native configuration meets the requirement or an app or integration needs to handle the remaining work.",
  faq: [
    { q: "Can a native workflow create a renewal deal?", a: "Yes. HubSpot documents native record creation, property copying and associations. Verify your account's workflow access and selected actions. Creating a deal does not by itself prove complete copying of negotiated line items or duplicate prevention." },
    { q: "When should we consider custom code or a clone app?", a: "When a tested native configuration cannot preserve the required source values or handle the agreed recovery and duplicate rules. Compare each route against the same example. HubSpot's custom-code workflow action requires Data Hub Professional or Enterprise; verify current access before selecting it." },
  ],
  ctaHeading: "Find the renewals that never got created",
  ctaBody: "The renewal audit finds the closed-won deals with no renewal built, and the revenue attached, against your real portal. $600, credited in full toward any build. It starts with a free 30-minute call.",
  relatedPost: { href: "/posts/hubspot-renewal-deal-workflow-automation/", label: "HubSpot renewal deal automation: specification and acceptance tests" },
};
---
<RenewalGuide {...props} />
